#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120630} :activity/id #uuid "5359b253-6e73-5b33-a1e8-93617de4d0e4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049875 :topic/id #uuid "1a256f47-ce78-5add-89be-3b134897d2ac" :topic/math-academy-id 1774} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121022 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n*The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/25/256531b7f0ddb14dd11a59a3019a3361f1607d3fe16fa6d530a461344de8f39e.svg) are both square matrices, then\n\n![](images/e5/e59b47ca5462a066093945e2698404e595a92e9e70a2b8c4f8b2f30df9c9fbf1.svg)\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) that is defined as follows:\n![](images/9a/9a705ea4bd482cdf83251288108b48ff5bc7354c9d51c1b710b9e734c8a32dea.svg)\n\nTo easily compute ![](images/da/dab9a65c1cba3cd9f271a1910b060e5428813107b3b54945776aab3839003d45.svg), we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n![](images/fa/fa4214541c12b0a761fd7cb17f4efc2f6b4be2c1424094483a3c2f61ab63c0a0.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a8abcbc-c41d-5553-8b2e-daa7256d7d34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3009 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "425217e0-d658-532e-a440-a3ddd4b953cd" :step/math-academy-id 16227 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"} :step/id #uuid "b7196473-0bc4-579e-90dc-f9136aa28e30" :step/math-academy-id 16228 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121023 :tutorial/content "Another useful property of determinants is that, for any invertible matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the above property is true, remember that $A \\cdot A^{-1} = I$ for any invertible matrix $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =I \\\\ det(A\\cdot {A}^{-1}) & =det(I) \\\\ det(A)\\cdot det({A}^{-1}) & =1 \\\\ det({A}^{-1}) & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** We can only apply the formula $\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}$ if $A$ is invertible, meaning that $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "043b8a4a-8f67-5ae8-a7ae-bbda3fa2bdbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3010 :tutorial/title "The Determinant of an Inverse Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c3a5d472-bd0b-516a-aa13-594c8fc6b508" :step/math-academy-id 16229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435770}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "89f23361-1b55-5691-bba9-28621e40e324" :step/math-academy-id 16230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\,\\,=det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f26cf5f4-fd6d-5502-94d6-4b34bd2568ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3011 :tutorial/title "The Linearity of the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "f7d6d5a0-3ce7-530f-a0b1-24773b682d6b" :step/math-academy-id 16231 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435768}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "eeaa9b6a-90e5-5a18-85ae-913e7cbc0f24" :step/math-academy-id 16232}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119996}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435774} :activity/id #uuid "a34c2423-d9d1-5a94-af8c-6a8b5fe0dba0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049875 :topic/id #uuid "1a256f47-ce78-5add-89be-3b134897d2ac" :topic/math-academy-id 1774} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd88020-b9de-5670-bbb4-d000faac032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435744} {:answer/id #uuid "4e375b53-cb2c-56d7-a604-46a74124343b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435749} {:answer/id #uuid "82e7b3e3-84d8-5737-a5f1-d3862a09a444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435753} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3611ee-496d-5cec-8534-0299f1c2f846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435756} {:answer/id #uuid "f62fda4e-f88c-56fa-9e53-1860b2a19543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "244" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435756} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7b949d-cbe6-5045-bf34-26b00edf4f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3797960f-4f46-583b-afe6-1bb724ee5f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130641" :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 103 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144+0 & 158+0 & 102+1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 144 & 158 & 102\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 144 & 158\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 158-4\\cdot 144) \\\\  & =-(316-576) \\\\  & =260\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d161349-e4e8-5aa1-b4e7-f6d11a6bf9dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74feda84-0372-5ba8-a03f-6504fdd43cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435751} {:answer/id #uuid "81e999d9-b74a-531e-b396-75bcda8940a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435752} {:answer/id #uuid "a5465cc4-707e-5513-9c9b-2c4ccb77d7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435754} {:answer/id #uuid "aee93a59-f631-5538-b707-3dbdac3a5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435757} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c0bd69-0ae8-5c67-b00c-53d8d88f988d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435752} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1881ff-14ad-5797-8c35-e1990ea0829e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "788f9347-638e-5e07-bc97-727179291d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-130780" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a-2 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{3}+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (5+1) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77ada6f1-1dfa-54e5-88ae-7663f6d4729f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358} {:answer/id #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353093} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} :answer-field/id #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a2caee0-7a00-5713-8017-f3895a8befc5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f82cc2-edfd-5f43-b0c8-b4fb5bfe4f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435746} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7dc16-cbeb-5e5b-b98b-3d2a1113c52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435747} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea38b1-c61b-5047-b0ee-8b524e948890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435755} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3a92c4-d4e6-5591-8bf1-5151ac26a42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435759} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f0546b-c6ef-592b-ad4b-1acba6597c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435747} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9759cd8-4862-5dd3-9edc-5966b55e2eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eee0d247-b841-57bb-b4de-7b3606da042f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42583" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(12-15)+2(14+15)+(-35-30) \\\\  & =3+58-65 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9f1a9a64-1470-537f-a2d2-3bc970633d21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d19261f-2d17-5ce6-a528-357db19c92fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{76}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435743} {:answer/id #uuid "44e2e6b4-ad39-5370-b46a-da247adce9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435745} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3f9d6-53b9-5b49-91f9-095f7560a365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435748} {:answer/id #uuid "505a34ac-ba5d-5a72-8645-7ed3b579499e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435750} {:answer/id #uuid "ce24b16c-5b82-5027-a711-29aa725ec9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435748} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f79099-495d-5b97-91da-18a1c01e2ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8501cdd8-7879-5e81-a6ba-962c1d7f9129" :question/math-academy-id "q-130532" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|+2(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-18+16)+(-30-4)-2(20+3) \\\\  & =-4-34-46 \\\\  & =-84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{84}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ab0d2d10-5ed3-5c47-8920-753336a27760" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435771}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435742}]}
